-- Q11283: Government Regulation of Business [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 11 of the Securities Act of 1933, investors can hold auditors liable as "experts" for material misstatements in audited financial statements included in the registration statement. The investor need not prove scienter or reliance, making this the strongest claim when the auditors acted negligently rather than intentionally.
Wrong (A): Rule 10b-5 requires proof of scienter (intent to deceive). Because the auditors acted negligently without intent, the investor cannot satisfy this element.
Wrong (B): Section 18 of the Exchange Act applies to documents filed with the SEC but requires proof of reliance, making it harder to establish than Section 11.
Wrong (C): Section 12(a)(2) applies to sellers and offerors in public offerings but does not typically reach auditors who are not in privity with the investor.$EXPL$
